{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 28, :comments ["<b>And Jacob lived in the land of Egypt, etc.</b> Why is this parashah closed? For once Jacob passed away, the enslavement began. Another matter: that he sought to reveal the End, and it was sealed from him. Another matter: that all the troubles in the world were sealed from him. Thus is the version in Bereshit Rabbah (96:1). According to what appears, there is no support from Scripture for all these derashot, and all of them are as if spoken prophetically. And what appears to me in this matter is that we find here a closed parashah unlike the manner of the closed parashiyot in the Torah, in which there is a space of at least the measure of a closed parashah — whereas here there is no space at all. Surely the intention of Ezra the Scribe was to join the verse 'וַיְחִי' to the verse preceding it, 'וַיֵּשֶׁב יִשְׂרָאֵל בְּאֶרֶץ גֹּשֶׁן וַיֵּאָחֲזוּ בָהּ וַיִּפְרוּ וַיִּרְבוּ מְאֹד' (Genesis 47:27), 'וַיְחִי יַעֲקֹב' etc. (Genesis 47:28), as if it were all one verse. Therefore these three formulations were divided according to the implications that the darshanim derive." "For according to the first formulation, which darshans that once Jacob passed away the enslavement began, the joining of these two verses will be on this pattern: for first it says, 'וַיֵּשֶׁב יִשְׂרָאֵל בְּאֶרֶץ גֹּשֶׁן' (Genesis 47:27), which with the language of 'dwelling' indicates that they had a dwelling of tranquility and rest; 'וַיֵּאָחֲזוּ' — that they also had a holding in the land; 'וַיִּפְרוּ וַיִּרְבוּ מְאֹד' — and all this was during the time of 'וַיְחִי יַעֲקֹב' (Genesis 47:28), for during his lifetime the merit of Jacob stood for them so that they merited all these — implying that at his death his merit ceased and everything ceased: for they no longer had a peaceful dwelling because of the enslavement, and certainly they had no holding in the land, for they were slaves; and likewise they were not permitted to be fruitful and multiply, for the Egyptians sought to diminish them through affliction, saying 'פֶּן יִרְבֶּה' (Exodus 1:10). And according to this, the death of Jacob was the cause of the enslavement." "And we may say the reverse of this: that the beginning of the enslavement was the cause of his death, for the Holy One, blessed be He, shortened his years so that he did not reach the days of his fathers — so that he would not see the enslavement of his children, for the time of 'וַעֲבָדוּם וְעִנּוּ אֹתָם' (Genesis 15:13) had arrived. Therefore it says, 'וַיִּקְרְבוּ יְמֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל לָמוּת,' and our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Bereshit Rabbah 96:4) that everyone of whom 'drawing near' is stated did not reach the days of his fathers. And in this manner the juxtaposition will be explained as well, just as in the first manner. And the number seventeen years that it specifies is to say that the recognition of Joseph, which took place when he was seventeen years old, caused them to dwell in peace in Egypt for only seventeen years." "And according to the third formulation, which darshans that all the troubles in the world were sealed from him, the verse would say: even though all his days were in trouble and sojourning, as it is written, 'יְמֵי מְגוּרַי שְׁלֹשִׁים וּשְׁלֹשִׁים שָׁנָה מְעַט וְרָעִים' (Genesis 47:9) — nevertheless, because of the greatness of the tranquility that he had during those seventeen years, when he saw his children dwelling in peace and they took hold in the land and were fruitful and multiplied — all the earlier troubles that had befallen him were forgotten from him and were as if they had never been. Therefore it says, 'וַיְהִי יְמֵי יַעֲקֹב שְׁנֵי חַיָּיו שֶׁבַע שָׁנִים וְאַרְבָּעִים וּמְאַת שָׁנָה' (Genesis 47:28), for those hundred and thirty years were not counted among the days of his life because of the greatness of his troubles, and now retroactively they were all counted as 'the years of his life.' And this is because all the troubles that had passed were sealed from him, as though he had lived a pleasant and sweet life in all his years — for in truth, through Joseph, he was alive. Therefore 'וַיְחִי יַעֲקֹב' (Genesis 47:28) was joined to the preceding verse. And what Rashi omitted of this formulation is because it appears to him that it is the same as the first formulation, as is clear to one who understands with a little reflection." "But as for the second formulation, which darshans that he sought to reveal the End and the Shekhinah departed from him — for it comes to resolve what is meant by the statement 'וַיְחִי יַעֲקֹב בְּאֶרֶץ מִצְרַיִם שְׁבַע עֶשְׂרֵה שָׁנָה' (Genesis 47:28): what does this teach us? Do we still not know that he was in Egypt seventeen years, for when he came before Pharaoh he said, 'יְמֵי מְגוּרַי שְׁלֹשִׁים וּמְאַת שָׁנָה' (Genesis 47:9), and if so, why was it necessary to say that he lived in Egypt seventeen years? And furthermore, why was this formulation not mentioned regarding Abraham and Isaac or the Matriarchs? But surely it comes to teach that the Shekhinah departed from him, for it was stated above, 'וַתְּחִי רוּחַ יַעֲקֹב אֲבִיהֶם' (Genesis 45:27), which means that the Shekhinah rested upon him, since it says 'רוּחַ יַעֲקֹב' — and one might have thought that the spirit of the Holy One, blessed be He, rested upon him all the days until the day of his death. It therefore teaches us: 'וַיְחִי יַעֲקֹב בְּאֶרֶץ מִצְרַיִם' — for when he came to Egypt, Jacob lived, but not 'רוּחַ יַעֲקֹב' — for the Shekhinah departed from him immediately, because he said, 'אָמוּתָה הַפַּעַם אַחֲרֵי רְאוֹתִי אֶת פָּנֶיךָ' (Genesis 46:30). And if so, he was immediately ready for the command to command [his sons] because of death, and the Holy One, blessed be He, was concerned lest he reveal the End; therefore the Shekhinah departed from him. But we still do not know for what reason the Shekhinah departed from him — therefore this parashah comes closed, to join it to the verse 'וַיֵּשֶׁב יִשְׂרָאֵל בְּאֶרֶץ גֹּשֶׁן' (Genesis 47:27), because it is well known that great harm follows from knowledge of the End, for the earlier generations who know that the redemption will not come in their days will not seek the face of HaShem to ask for the redemption and its hastening." "And similarly the Akeidah explained, regarding the midrash (Yalkut Shimoni 157) that says: when Jacob sought to reveal the End, the Holy One, blessed be He, said, ״וְלֹא אוֹתִי קָרָאתָ יַעֲקֹב״ (Isaiah 43:22) — meaning that he did not have regard for My honor, for from this knowledge this harm would ensue: that the earlier generations would not call upon Me nor seek My face, and they would seek to settle permanently in the lands of the nations, to acquire for themselves a holding in their lands as resident aliens and as those who despair of redemption. Therefore HaShem sealed and concealed the final End, so that in every generation they would seek the face of HaShem and David their king, and would always await the End of His salvation. As, in our iniquity, this trait is found among us even in a time when the End is not known — nonetheless, many are the common folk of the land who settle in the lands of the nations and build for themselves luxurious and esteemed houses, built to last of stone; and for this reason they never seek the face of HaShem with all their heart to bring them to their land — therefore the Holy One, blessed be He, leaves them there. And to this matter the verse ״וַיֵּשֶׁב יִשְׂרָאֵל בְּאֶרֶץ גֹּשֶׁן״ (Genesis 47:27) speaks as an indictment of Israel: that they sought to be permanent residents and to acquire for themselves a holding in a land not theirs. And this harm came to them because the End of the Egyptian exile was known to them. Therefore the early generations, who knew clearly that the redemption would not be in their days, sought for themselves permanent settlement and a holding in the land of Egypt. Therefore Scripture juxtaposed to this verse the verse ״וַיְחִי יַעֲקֹב״ (Genesis 47:28), to teach that this harm, which was drawn upon those generations because their End was known to them, caused ״וַיְחִי יַעֲקֹב״ — but not the spirit of Jacob. For Jacob alone remained alive, so that he would not reveal the final End and so that this harm would not spread to the generations."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 47, :notes {5 "The citation ילקו״ש קנז מט resolved as Yalkut Shimoni, section 157, on the verse מט (corresponding to the ל״ט numerical value indicator)."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:34:27.459442153Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}